    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, anchored by **Trea Turner** (78.26) and **Kyle Schwarber** (74.75). Both benefit significantly from a favorable matchup against Sugano, with **Trea Turner** seeing a massive 18% boost due to the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill profile and his prime position at the top of the order. **Yordan Alvarez** (72.28) remains a must-start despite the platoon disadvantage against Abbott; his elite 0.428 xwOBA is simply too potent to bench, even when the matchup dynamics slightly dampen his projection. For owners hunting the scarce stolen base category, **Caleb Durbin** (62.99) and **Dillon Dingler** (62.05) are our high-leverage plays today. **Caleb Durbin** benefits from winds blowing out and an advantageous SP matchup, making him a critical engine for our SB production despite hitting in the nine-hole.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Chase Meidroth** (37.94), who fell just shy of our 40.0 efficiency floor. While **Chase Meidroth** offered some utility, he was squeezed out by the daunting matchup against Gilbert, whose 3.09 xERA punished his final score. We are playing it safe with our roster management to preserve game caps, effectively benching **Chase Meidroth** to avoid a sub-par performance. We are also monitoring **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (52.12) closely; while his speed is vital, he faces the elite arm of deGrom, which suppresses his ceiling significantly. The risk profile is heightened for **Carson Benge** (47.24), who is fighting a stiff platoon disadvantage against Rodriguez, making him a candidate for an early pinch-hit appearance should the game flow shift. We remain without **Christian Yelich**, **Zac Veen**, and **Colt Keith**, whose absences continue to necessitate tactical depth from the likes of **Gary Sanchez**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Luke Raley**, and **Junior Caminero**.",
